1
00:00:00,125 --> 00:00:01,435
How much more rope?

2
00:00:01,845 --> 00:00:03,200
Round the whole Earth,

3
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:05,720
lifted 1 meter
off the ground.

4
00:00:06,925 --> 00:00:09,695
Start with a rope
that hugs the planet,

5
00:00:09,705 --> 00:00:12,860
about 40,000 kilometers
long.

6
00:00:12,990 --> 00:00:15,760
Now lift it 1 meter
above the ground,

7
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:16,510
everywhere.

8
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:20,855
A whole planet of rope,
lifted all at once.

9
00:00:20,855 --> 00:00:22,110
You might guess it needs

10
00:00:22,110 --> 00:00:24,495
thousands
of kilometers more.

11
00:00:25,525 --> 00:00:29,105
But a circle's length
is two times pi times

12
00:00:29,105 --> 00:00:32,215
its radius, the distance
to its center.

13
00:00:32,695 --> 00:00:34,315
Lift the rope a meter,

14
00:00:34,315 --> 00:00:36,480
and the radius
grows a meter.

15
00:00:39,325 --> 00:00:42,275
So the rope grows
by two times pi

16
00:00:42,275 --> 00:00:45,685
times 1 meter,
a bit over 6 meters.

17
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:47,560
That is about
four of you,

18
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:49,050
lying head to toe.

19
00:00:52,525 --> 00:00:55,220
Try a basketball
or the Sun:

20
00:00:55,220 --> 00:00:57,150
lift the rope 1 meter,

21
00:00:57,150 --> 00:00:59,715
and the extra
rope is the same.

22
00:01:00,320 --> 00:01:02,560
The planet's size
cancels out:

23
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:04,300
only the lift matters.

24
00:01:04,575 --> 00:01:08,200
10 times higher needs
10 times more rope.

25
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:11,285
Next envelope:
a shuffled deck,

26
00:01:11,285 --> 00:01:13,045
and a very rare order.
